+
+Beyond this equilibrium limit, the chapter introduces a teleodynamic curvature tensor $R^{(\text{pref})}_{ijkl}$, built from the preference metric's own Riemann tensor in the ordinary way, which has no counterpart in Book 6's treatment: where that book's curvature was built directly from entropy and potential gradients via $K_{ij}$, the present tensor is built from the second derivatives of $\log P_{\text{steady}}$ itself, giving a genuinely distinct measure of how strongly a system's preferences bend its own configuration space. An example developed here maps a decision manifold, in which $R^{(\text{pref})}_{ijkl}$ feeds back into the preference potential that generates it, illustrating a case in which valuation and geometry co-determine one another rather than one simply producing the other.
